     Another very obvious feature of the sky is the culmination point. If
you face either south or north and imagine a line passing directly
overhead cutting the sky in two, you will be imagining what is called the
prime meridian. Where this line cuts the ecliptic is the current MC, or
Midheaven. Stars anywhere along this line are culminating, reaching the
top of their rising arc, and are about to start traveling down toward the
western horizon. In figure 5 (p. 17), stars A and B are both culminating
but star B is at a higher altitude than A.
