Sun, having reached its southernmost point of rising on the horizon,
would turn on its path and begin its journey northward. It was the
Chaldeans who officially linked this constellation to the Crab, apparently
because   of this   sideways   movement of the                 Sun   as   it entered this
constellation.— See Star Map 20. below.
     The Egyptians, however, linked the constellation to the Scarab
Beetle (see figure 21. p. 254), a symbol of immortality, rising from the
dead and settling "upon the empty throne in the boat of Ra,"— a place
where the Sun god was bom again. This may well be the source for the
later belief that this group of stars was the source of all life and a portal,
the place where human souls entered into the Earth's material realms. In
contrast, the stars of Capricorn, the winter solstice, were seen as the

place of departure or death.—


